'''Kafto-Baliscans''' ({{lang-bl|Kafto-baliscano}}; {{Langx|mxi|اَلْكَفْتُو بَالِسْكَايِينْ|Al-Kaftu-Baliskayén}}) are a [[W:Panethnicity|pan-ethnic]] group in the [[Baliscan Confederation]], consisting of residents of Balisca of full or partial [[Kaftian diaspora|Kaftian descent]] (covering [[W:List of ethnic groups of Africa|thousands]] of diverse ethnic groups), inclusive of [[List of regions of Kaftia|all regions of Kaftia]] (including [[South Kaftia]]). According to the [[2025 Baliscan census]], people solely identifying as Kafto-Baliscans constitute roughly 39.78% of the Baliscan population. Additionally, when taking into account the number of people that also identify with [[Mestizo Baliscans|multiple ethnicities]], this percentage rises to 46.14% of the total population. 

Kaftian were dispersed throughout the [[Baliscan archipelago]] as a result of the [[Ethiopian slave trade|Ethiopian]], [[Trans-Kaftian slave trade|Trans-Kaftian]], and [[Kendriyan slave trade|Kendriyan]] slave trades in the [[Colonial Balisca|colonial era]]. During the slavery period between the 16th and the first decade of 19th centuries, Balisca received approximately one to three million enslaved [[W:Black people|Black Kaftians]], who constituted about 20% of all enslaved people brought to the Algarbes. Many Kaftians who escaped slavery fled to ''[[W:Quilombo|quilombos/palenques]]'', communities where they could live freely and resist oppression, eventually coalescing into the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. [[Maroon Wars|Militant opposition]] to slavery precipitated the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] and the proliferation of [[Maroon pirates|Maroon piracy]]. In 1808, the [[Granyana Convention]] determined the definitive prohibition of the transatlantic slave trade and abolition of slavery across the [[Southern Confederation|Northern Confederation]] with the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]]. 

Depending on the definition, Kafto-Baliscans are the largest [[W:Panethnic|pan-ethnic]] group in the Baliscan Confederation. According to census data from [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS), Kaftian migrants generally do not [[W:Self-concept|self-identify]] as Kafto-Baliscan. The overwhelming majority of Kaftian immigrants identify with their own respective ethnicities (≈95%). The Kafto-Baliscan population constitutes the largest concentration of Kaftian-descended people in the world, numbering between 61 and 71 million Baliscans. With the largest Kaftian-descendant population outside of Kaftia, Balisca's cultural, social, and economic landscape has been profoundly shaped by Kafto-Baliscans, with especially notable contributions in religion ([[Lucumí Tarica]]), sports, cuisine, literature, music, and dance, with elements like [[W:Samba|samba]] and [[W:Capoeira|capoeira]] reflecting their heritage.

== History ==

=== Colonial Balisca ===
{{Main|Maroon Wars}}
[[File:Catro Mullers Granata.png|left|thumb|''Catro Mullers Granata'' ("Four Maroon Women") by [[Elies Serramitjans]] (1632)]]
In 1521, Volisanian colonial authorities, relying heavily on Bayani and Sanyanian slave traders, passed a series of ordinances to limit the number of Muslim Kaftians taken to their colonies in Abyala. Many of these Kaftians were captives of war with military experience, and they were viewed as particularly dangerous. The Volisanian colonies' desire for slave labor outweighed their fears, which were well-founded as enslaved Kaftians (Muslims and non-Muslims alike) continued to rebel. Over the next three centuries there would continue to be violent instances of revolts in Bayara, preventing their settlement expansion further into the interior from the coast.

Collectively, the Maroon Wars became the first and only slave uprising that led to the founding of a state which was both free from {{W|Slavery in the Americas|slavery}} and ruled by non-Illypnians and former captives. It is now widely seen as a defining moment in the history of the [[Ethiopic world]], particularly in [[Abyala]]. The early successes of the revolts had a long-lasting impact on the history of the Araucarian territory and the history of Balisca, determining the shape of future colonial relations with self-liberated Kaftian and Indigenous peoples, e.g. by causing the consolidation of the Araucarian [[W:Frontier|frontier]]. The effects of these conflicts on the institution of slavery were felt throughout the Algarbes. The disunity of the {{W|Peninsular War|besieged}} colonial government, and the ''de facto'' abolition of slavery in the colony was followed by a successful defense of the freedoms the former slaves won and, with the collaboration of already previously {{W|free people of color}}, solidified their independence from white Europeans.

==== Maroon Confederacy ====
{{Main|Maroon Confederacy}}
[[Daramazán]] was founded as a colony 1617 by escaped Morisco convicts and enslaved Kaftians, at a time when the Araucaria Territory remained largely unexplored by Illypnians. As a refuge for those fleeing the colonial authorities, the territory became notorious for its gaudy displays of wealth and loose morals. It was a popular homeport for Maroon pirates from which to launch their raids on Iberian slave ships and settlements to liberate enslaved people, most of whom then joined the colony. The outbreak of slave revolts against the Volisanians was to an extent supported by the colony, which helped to smuggle individuals along the Araucarian Passage. This city later joined with the other maroon settlements to form the Confederacy.

Eight Volisanian naval expeditions invaded the territory in an attempt to conquer it between 1699 and 1804, but these all failed with considerable loss of life as a result of violent guerrilla tactics and a lack of familiarity with the land. The Confederacy later intervened in the [[Baliscan Civil War]], playing a decisive role in beating back the [[Siege of Castejón]], and in subsequent years developed an amiable relationship with [[Anyos Federales|Reconstructionist Balisca]] with the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]]. The Confederacy continued to exercise sovereignty until it confederated with the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] at the [[1883 Confederal Convention]].

== Terminology ==

=== Black ===
{{Further|w:Black people}}
The Baliscan census, conducted by the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] does not collect data on [[W:Skin color|skin color]] or the concept of "[[W:Race (human categorization)|race]]" of respondents. As such, self-identification as black or ''Black Baliscan'' rather than ethnolinguistic heritage is seen not as a demographic fact, but an endorsement of [[W:Scientific racism|racial essentialism]] and the [[Kaftia#Sudan|racialized concept of Sudan]].

Despite this, in the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]], it was determined that 42,827,591 Baliscans self-identified as black, constituting 27.71% of the total Baliscan population and 69.66% of the total Kafto-Baliscan population.

